    17	In plain terms:
    18	- LV's §6 was written for degree d ≍ (log N)^{1/3} and heights L(1/3). Our degree is **fixed** (d = 9), and that
    19	  makes everything easier. Every field-dependent quantity (discriminants, the index, the class number, the number
    20	  of bad primes) is N^{O(1)}. The obstruction group H therefore has dimension O(log N), and O(log N) character
    21	  columns suffice.
    22	- The height V = N^{1/(4k)} enters only through bit sizes. Each atom has O(log N) bits, and the element whose
    23	  square root is taken has O(rows·log N) bits, instead of LV's O(rows·(log N)^{1/3}). Square roots cost
    24	  quasi-linear time in that size, so this is harmless.
    25	- The square root needs no inert prime and no lattice reduction. We Hensel-lift at **one** unramified prime
    26	  p ∈ (B, 2B] and try the 2^{τ−1} ≤ 2^{d−1} sign patterns. Each candidate is checked exactly (η² = Γ). Fixed d makes
    27	  this rigorous and cheap.
    28	- Siegel zeros are handled as in LV (Stark plus Landau), but at fixed d they cost even less: **at most one**
    29	  nonzero square class can escape the characters, for every field. Whenever the characters separate (probability
    30	  ≥ 1 − (log N)^{−2}), one of the three dependencies s₁, s₂, s₁ + s₂ is a square. Exact verification makes this
    31	  Las Vegas. This is a direct specialisation of LV's Lemma 6.10 to one conductor range (codex). LV had
    32	  O(log log N) exceptional classes and an exceptional set of fields.
