   149	## 3. Proof
   150	
   151	### 3.1 Columns: Lemma C (good primes)
   152	
   153	**Lemma C [proved].** Let r be a prime with r ∤ D, and let (a, b) be coprime with F(a, b) ≠ 0. Then:
   154	- (i) if r | b, then r ∤ F(a, b), and a − bα is a unit at every prime of O_K above r;
   155	- (ii) if r ∤ b, the only prime 𝔭 of O_K above r with ord_𝔭(a − bα) ≠ 0 is the first-degree prime
   156	  𝔭 = (r, β − t·ab^{−1})O_K (the kernel of α ↦ ab^{−1} on the r-integral elements). It occurs only if r | F(a, b),
   157	  and then ord_𝔭(a − bα) = v_r(F(a, b)).
   158	
   159	*Proof.*
   160	- Since r ∤ t, α is r-integral. Z_(r)[α] has discriminant disc(f)/t^{2d−2} up to an r-unit, which is prime to r.
   161	  So Z_(r)[α] is the maximal order at r. By Dedekind–Kummer the primes above r correspond to the irreducible
   162	  factors φ_j of f mod r (locally 𝔭_j = (r, φ_j(α)); globally, 𝔭_j = (r, t^{deg φ_j}φ_j(β/t))O_K), and all are
   163	  unramified.
   164	- (i) If r | b, then r ∤ a, and F(a, b) ≡ t a^d ≢ 0 (mod r). Also a − bα ≡ a (mod 𝔭) for every 𝔭 | r.
   165	- (ii) a − bα ∈ 𝔭_j iff φ_j(ab^{−1}) ≡ 0, i.e. iff φ_j = x − ab^{−1}, which is a first-degree prime 𝔭.
   166	  - Norm_{K/Q}(a − bα) = F(a, b)/t, and 𝔭 is the only prime above r dividing a − bα.
   167	  - Since f(𝔭/r) = 1, ord_𝔭(a − bα) = v_r(F(a, b)/t) = v_r(F(a, b)). ∎
   168	- Primes r > B with r ∤ D contribute nothing: r ∤ F(a, b) because F(a, b) is B-smooth.
   169	- Hence, for any S whose algebraic (r, s)-columns sum to zero mod 2:
   170	  ord_𝔓(γ_S) ≡ 0 (mod 2) for every prime 𝔓 of O_K not above a prime dividing D, where γ_S := ∏_P (a_P − b_Pα)^{e_P(S)}.
   171	- Bad primes need no columns. Neither the index [O_K : Z[β]] nor the factorization of D is ever computed.
   172	- (Tested: `test_good_prime_columns_lemma_C`.)
   173	
   174	**Rational side.** If the sign, the p-parity (p ≤ B) and the E-parity columns of S sum to zero, then
   175	q_S = g₁^{E(S)}·∏_P n_P^{−e_P(S)} (sign +1) is a square in Q^×, with explicit square root
   176	√q_S = g₁^{E/2} ∏_{p≤B} p^{−(Σ_P e_P v_p(n_P))/2}.
   177	- The **one** parity column also gives E(S) even, which makes t^{−E(S)} a square.
   178	- So γ_S = t^{−E(S)}·∏_P z_P^{e_P(S)} is a square in K iff γ′_S := ∏_P z_P^{e_P(S)} is.
   179	- This is the "g₁ column" of pcest §5. It counts atomic relations with multiplicity, and it handles f_d as well.
   180	  It is BLP §12.7's "condition that #S be even … taken care of by the last coordinate".
   181	
   182	### 3.2 The residual square-class group: Lemma H
   183	
   184	Let S_D be the set of primes of O_K above rational primes dividing D. Define
   185	  V_D := {γ ∈ K^× : ord_𝔓(γ) even for all 𝔓 ∉ S_D},   H := V_D/K^{×2}.
   186	By §3.1, every S in the kernel of the non-character columns has γ_S ∈ V_D. Write φ(S) := [γ_S] ∈ H. Then φ is
   187	F₂-linear on the kernel, because γ_{S+S′} = γ_Sγ_{S′}/γ_{S∩S′}².
   188	
   189	**Lemma H [proved; standard].** |H| ≤ 2^{|S_D|}·|Cl(K)[2]|·2^{r₁+r₂} ≤ 2^{d·ω(D) + d}·h_K. With the bound of
   190	Lenstra (Bull. AMS 26 (1992), Thm 6.5) applied to M_K ≤ √|Δ_K| ≤ √|disc g|, this gives
   191	  log₂|H| ≤ d·log₂|D| + d + ½log₂|disc g| + (d−1)·log₂(d + log₂|disc g|) = O_d(log N).
   192	*Proof.*
   193	- Put W := {γ : γO_K = 𝔞²} and Y := O_K^×K^{×2}. Then V_D ⊃ W ⊃ Y ⊃ K^{×2}.
   194	- The map V_D → ⊕_{𝔓∈S_D} Z/2, γ ↦ (ord_𝔓 γ mod 2), has kernel W.
   195	- The map W → Cl(K)[2], γ ↦ [𝔞], is well defined (the ideal group is torsion-free) and has kernel Y.
   196	- Y/K^{×2} ≅ O_K^×/O_K^{×2} has order 2^{r₁+r₂} ≤ 2^d (Dirichlet; roots of unity ±1 for odd d).
   197	- |S_D| ≤ d·ω(D) ≤ d·log₂|D|.
   198	- Sizes. ‖f‖_∞ ≤ C_d·g₁H = N^{O(1)}, so |disc f| ≤ d^{2d}‖f‖^{2d−2} and log|D|, log|disc g| = O_d(log N). ∎
   199	- **Attribution.** This is BLP's Thm 6.7 (LNM 1554, 1993) with the index [O_K : Z[α]] replaced by 2^{|S_D|}.
   200	  BLP cite the same class-number bound ("[27, Theorem 6.5, Remark]"). Lenstra's Thm 6.5 is stated with
   201	  (2/π)^{r₂}√|Δ|; the Minkowski-constant form is the Remark after it.
   202	- **Fallback without the citation.** Every ideal class contains an integral ideal of norm ≤ M_K. The number of
   203	  integral ideals of norm ≤ M_K is ≤ M_K(1 + ln M_K)^{d−1} (a divisor-function count). This gives the same
   204	  O_d(log N).
   205	- **Compared with LV (Claim 6.5).**
   206	  - LV use BLP's [V : W] ≤ [O_K : Z[f_dα]]. We use the cruder 2^{|S_D|}, which needs no l-functions on the
   207	    order, no projective-root bookkeeping, and no index computation.
   208	  - Units, class-group 2-torsion, the index and the non-monic leading coefficient are all inside H. None of them
   209	    is assumed trivial.
   210	  - The cost is a constant factor in the dimension: O(log N) either way.
   211	- (`numfield.class_group_log2_bound` computes this bound; E1 reports 271–604 bits for the toy fields.)
   212	
   213	### 3.3 Character columns: Lemma X
   214	
   215	For 0 ≠ h ∈ H, let L_h := K(√h), so [L_h : Q] = 2d.
   216	
   217	**Discriminant bound [proved].** |Δ_{L_h}| ≤ Δ̄ := |disc g|²·8^d·|D|^d.
   218	- Δ_{L_h} = Δ_K²·N_{K/Q}(𝔡_{L_h/K}).
   219	- Take a representative γ ∈ V_D. At 𝔓 ∤ 2D, ord_𝔓γ is even, so after removing a local square L_h/K is unramified
   220	  at 𝔓.
   221	- At 𝔓 | D, 𝔓 ∤ 2: ord_𝔓 𝔡 ≤ 1. At 𝔓 | 2: ord_𝔓 𝔡 ≤ 2·ord_𝔓(2) + 1.
   222	- So N(𝔡) ≤ 8^d·∏_{𝔓|D} N𝔓 ≤ 8^d |D|^d, and |Δ_K| ≤ |disc g|. Then log Δ̄ = O_d(log N).
   223	- For the quadratic subfield F of L_h (if any): |Δ_F|^d divides Δ_{L_h}, so |Δ_F| ≤ Δ̄^{1/d}.
   224	- No small representative of h is needed. LV's minimal-norm representative argument is not used.
   225	
   226	**Exceptional classes.** Let c_L > 0 be the absolute constant in Landau's theorem below. Put
   227	  ν₁ := min( 1/(4·(2d)!·log Δ̄), c_L/(2 log Δ̄) ).
   228	(Two quadratic conductors ≤ Δ̄^{1/d} have log(q₁q₂) ≤ (2/d) log Δ̄ < 2 log Δ̄, so 1 − ν₁ lies strictly inside
   229	Landau's interval.)
   230	and let E := {0 ≠ h ∈ H : ζ_{L_h} has a real zero β with 1 − ν₁ ≤ β < 1}.
   231	
   232	**Lemma E [proved from cited results]. |E| ≤ 1, for every field.**
   233	- **Cited inputs.**
   234	  - Stark (Invent. Math. 23 (1974), Lemma 8): if a number field L has a real zero β of ζ_L with
   235	    1 − (c(L) log|Δ_L|)^{−1} ≤ β < 1, where c(L) = 4[L:Q]! for non-normal L, then ζ_F(β) = 0 for some quadratic
   236	    F ⊂ L.
   237	  - Landau (1918; Davenport, *Multiplicative Number Theory*, Ch. 14; LV Fact 6.24): there is an effective absolute
   238	    c_L > 0 such that, for distinct real primitive characters χ₁, χ₂ of conductors q₁, q₂ with real zeros β₁ of
   239	    L(s, χ₁) and β₂ of L(s, χ₂), min(β₁, β₂) < 1 − c_L/log(q₁q₂). This form covers β₁ = β₂.
   240	- *Proof.*
   241	  - Let h ∈ E with zero β. Since ν₁ ≤ 1/(4(2d)! log Δ_{L_h}), Stark gives a quadratic F_h ⊂ L_h with
   242	    ζ_{F_h}(β) = ζ(β)L(β, χ_{F_h}) = 0. Since ζ(β) ≠ 0 on (0, 1), L(β, χ_{F_h}) = 0.
   243	  - [L_h : Q] = 2d with d odd, so L_h has at most one quadratic subfield: two would generate a biquadratic subfield
   244	    of degree 4 ∤ 2d. K has none, so L_h = K·F_h.
   245	  - h ↦ L_h is injective (Kummer theory), hence so is h ↦ F_h.
   246	  - |Δ_{F_h}| ≤ |Δ_{L_h}|^{1/d} ≤ Δ̄^{1/d}. Two distinct h, h′ ∈ E would give distinct real primitive characters
   247	    with zeros ≥ 1 − ν₁ > 1 − c_L/log(|Δ_{F_h}||Δ_{F_{h′}}|), i.e. two zeros (with multiplicity) in Landau's
