   329	### 3.5 Square root in K: Algorithm SQ and Lemma SQ
   330	
   331	**Algorithm SQ.**
   332	- **Input.**
   333	  - Γ ∈ Z[β], given as g′(β)² times a product of atoms z_P (with multiplicities).
   334	  - An integer A with this property: if Γ = η² for some η ∈ Z[β], then ‖η‖_∞ ≤ A.
   335	- **Steps.**
   336	  1. Let p be the least prime in (B, 2B] with p ∤ D. It exists because (B, 2B] has ≫ B/log B primes and
   337	     ω(D) = O(log N). It is found by sieving (B, 2B] once per field, in Õ(B) = L^{a+o(1)} time. (Alternatively reuse
   338	     a character prime r_j > Δ̄.) Then g mod p is squarefree, and Γ is a unit at p, because Norm(Γ) = ±disc(g)²·∏(t^{d−1}F(a_P, b_P))^{|e_P|}
   339	     has only prime factors ≤ B or dividing D.
   340	  2. Factor ḡ = φ₁⋯φ_τ over F_p (τ ≤ d), Las Vegas. In each F_p[y]/φ_j, test whether Γ is a square (Euler) and take
   341	     a square root ρ_j (Tonelli–Shanks in F_{p^{deg φ_j}}, Las Vegas). If some component is a non-square, output
   342	     NONSQUARE.
   343	  3. For each of the 2^{τ−1} sign vectors ε with ε₁ = +1:
   344	     - CRT the values ε_jρ_j to ρ_ε ∈ Z[β]/p.
   345	     - Starting from z₀ := ρ_ε^{−1}, Newton-lift the inverse square root, z ↦ z + z(1 − Γz²)/2 mod p^{2^i}, to the
   346	       first precision p^e > 2A.
   347	     - Put η_ε := Γz mod p^e with centred coefficients. If η_ε² = Γ exactly, output η_ε.
   348	  4. Output NONSQUARE.
   349	
   350	**Lemma SQ [proved].**
   351	- (i) **Correctness.**
   352	  - Any output η satisfies η² = Γ exactly.
   353	  - If Γ ∈ K^{×2}, then Γ = η² with η ∈ Z[β], and SQ outputs ±η.
   354	- (ii) **The bound A.** Let Mh := ⌊‖g‖₂⌋ + 1 (≥ Mahler(g) ≥ max_k max(1, |β_k|)) and g_d := d‖g‖₁Mh^{d−1}. Then
   355	  A := d·⌈U^{1/2}⌉·2^{d−1}Mh·g_d^{d−1}, U := g_d²·∏_P (‖z_P‖₁Mh^{d−1})^{|e_P|},
   356	  is valid. log₂A ≤ ½·Σ_P|e_P|·log₂(‖z_P‖₁Mh^{d−1}) + O(d² log(d‖g‖₁Mh)) = O_d((1 + Σ|e_P|)·log N).
   357	  (The numerator estimate uses the product defining the Mahler measure:
   358	  |[y^j]∏_{ℓ≠k}(y − β_ℓ)| ≤ C(d−1, j)·Mahler(g) ≤ 2^{d−1}Mh.)
   359	- (iii) **Cost.** The cost is O_d(𝖬(log A)·log log A) + (log N)^{O(1)} bit operations, where 𝖬 is integer
   360	  multiplication time. This is quasi-linear in the size of Γ.
   361	
   362	*Proof.*
   363	- **(i).**
   364	  - Write Γ = g′(β)²·γ with γ := ∏ z_P^{|e_P|} ∈ Z[β].
   365	  - If Γ = δ², δ ∈ K, then (δ/g′(β))² = γ ∈ O_K, so δ/g′(β) ∈ O_K.
   366	  - g′(β)O_K ⊂ Z[β]: indeed O_K ⊂ O_K^∨ ⊂ Z[β]^∨ = g′(β)^{−1}Z[β] (Euler's lemma; Z[β] ⊂ O_K). Hence
   367	    η := δ ∈ Z[β].
   368	  - p ∤ disc(g), so Z_p[β] ≅ ∏_j Z_p[y]/(G_j) is a product of unramified local rings, with G_j the Hensel lifts of
   369	    the φ_j.
   370	  - η mod p equals ±ρ_ε for exactly one class ε with ε₁ = +1.
   371	  - For p odd and Γ a unit, a unit square root with prescribed residue lifts uniquely (Hensel). So
   372	    η_ε ≡ ±η (mod p^e). Since p^e > 2A ≥ 2‖η‖_∞, centring recovers ±η exactly.
   373	  - Conversely, the check η_ε² = Γ is exact. NONSQUARE at step 2 is correct because η ∈ O_K would reduce to a
   374	    square in O_K/𝔓 = F_p[y]/φ_j (p is prime to the index).
   375	- **(ii).**
   376	  - |σ_k(z)| ≤ ‖z‖₁Mh^{d−1} and |g′(β_k)| ≤ g_d, so |σ_k(η)|² ≤ U.
   377	  - Coefficients come from the inverse Vandermonde: (V^{−1})_{jk} = [y^j](g(y)/(y − β_k))/g′(β_k).
   378	    - The numerator coefficients are ≤ 2^{d−1}Mh.
   379	    - ∏_k |g′(β_k)| = |disc g| ≥ 1 gives |g′(β_k)| ≥ g_d^{−(d−1)}.
   380	- **(iii).**
   381	  - Γ is computed by a product tree.
   382	  - Newton doubles the precision. Each step is O(d²) multiplications of ≤ log₂(p^e) = O(log A) bits.
   383	  - The sign enumeration multiplies by 2^{d−1} = O(1). ∎
   384	- (E1: all 92 NONSQUARE outputs were independently certified, and all 116 squares were verified. The ratio
   385	  log₂‖η‖/log₂A was 0.08–0.22, so A is conservative but polynomially tight.)
   386	- **Prior art (literature critic).** Algorithm SQ is the p-adic method of BLP §9 (LNM 1554, pp. 71–75).
   387	  - BLP already Newton-iterate the inverse square root to twice a proved coefficient bound.
   388	  - They optionally verify exactly ("free ourselves from … the unproved conjecture of Section 8").
   389	  - At a squarefree, non-inert prime they use the 2^t − 1 starting values, after Weinberger–Rothschild (ACM TOMS 2
   390	    (1976)).
   391	  - What is ours: the explicit bound A for the non-monic Z[β], the Hensel-prime choice and the cost at our heights.
   392	- Couveignes (LNM 1554) needs odd degree and inert primes. Montgomery's and Nguyen's methods are lattice-based
   393	  and heuristic.
   394	- LV cite Montgomery "in time O(M²)" without a rigorous analysis. Lemma SQ, that is BLP §9 made explicit,
   395	  replaces that step.
