   438	## 4. Field-level events, Lemma IR, and what changes in pcest
   439	
   440	The kept field of hypD Corollary K has law ∝ q_A, which amplifies the probability of a field event by at most
   441	1/μ, with μ ≥ (γ/2)^{2k+1}L^{−o(1)} = L^{−o(1)}. We instead **reject** bad fields before any descent attempt.
   442	- **Scope.** The bounds below use the parameter range of Theorem A (θ < 1, N large): then 0 < |t| < N, content < N
   443	  and 2H + 1 < N.
   444	- **Order of the argument (made precise after codex lv6_2).**
   445	  1. FM, Theorem R, Lemma Kern, Prop 4.2 and Theorem 4.3 are applied **unchanged, to the original law 𝒜**. Under
   446	     LD they give D_k(γ) for 𝒜.
   447	  2. Factor-producing events (a proper gcd, a reducible f with a proper factor F₁(g₀, g₁)) end the algorithm
   448	     successfully. They are not "rejections".
   449	  3. Let 𝓡 be the remaining rejection event: N | f′(m), or f reducible without a proper factor. Then
   450	     E_𝒜[g_A·1_{𝓡ᶜ}] ≥ γ − Pr_𝒜[𝓡], and Pr_𝒜[𝓡] = L^{−ω(1)} = o(γ).
   451	  4. Theorem K is invoked for the law 𝒜 conditioned on "not rejected" (restricted to non-terminating fields). This
   452	     law has D_k with γ/2 for large N.
   453	- These statements do not assert that FM etc. hold verbatim for the conditioned law. They do not need to.
   454	
   455	| event | bound | treatment |
   456	|---|---|---|
   457	| gcd(g₁, N) ≠ 1 | — | proper factor (0 < g₁ < N) |
   458	| degree drop f_d = 0 | **impossible**: f_d ≡ a_d ≡ N g₀^{−d} ≢ 0 (mod g₁) (codex) | — |
   459	| gcd(f_d, N) ≠ 1 | — | proper factor (0 < \|f_d\| < N) |
   460	| content(f) > 1 | content divides F(g₀, g₁) = N and is < N | proper factor |
   461	| f reducible | ≤ exp(−log H/(13d log log H)) (Lemma IR) | factor f over Z (LLL 1982, poly time); if some F_j(g₀, g₁) gives a proper factor, output it; otherwise reject. Simpler (proof critic): accept f only if some r ∈ 𝒫 gives an irreducible degree-d reduction, which certifies irreducibility; the rejection probability is the same bound |
   462	| N \| f′(g₀/g₁) | ≤ 1/(2H+1) | reject (a proper gcd is a factor) |
   463	| bad primes, index, units, Cl[2] | no rarity needed | inside H (Lemma H) |
   464	| exceptional zero | ≤ 1 class, every field | Lemma E, three tests |
   465	| characters miss | ≤ (log N)^{−2} per attempt | resample |
   466	| Wiedemann / finite-field roots | Las Vegas | retry |
   467	
   468	**N | f′(m) [proved].** Put m = g₀/g₁ mod N, so g₁m ≡ g₀.
   469	- f′ = F̂′ + g₁R + (g₁x − g₀)R′, so f′(m) ≡ f̂′(m) + g₁R_c(m) (mod N).
   470	- R_c(m) = Σ_i c_i m^{d−1−i} has coefficient 1 at c_{d−1}, and g₁ is a unit.
   471	- So for fixed c₀..c_{d−2}, at most one value of c_{d−1} ∈ [−H, H] (since 2H + 1 < N) gives N | f′(m). ∎
   472	
   473	**Lemma IR (irreducibility) [proved; elementary].** Fix g with gcd(g₁, N) = 1 and N free of primes ≤ B. For c
   474	uniform in C_H,
   475	  Pr[f reducible over Q] ≤ e·(1 − 1/(4d))^{|𝒫|},
   476	where 𝒫 is the set of primes in (r₀(d), ½ log H]. Hence the bound is ≤ exp(−log H/(13 d log log H)) for large N.
   477	*Proof.*
   478	- Let Q := ∏_{r∈𝒫} r ≤ H^{1/2+o(1)} ≤ 2H/d.
   479	- **Near-uniformity.** Each residue class of c mod Q has between ⌊(2H+1)/Q⌋^d and ⌈(2H+1)/Q⌉^d elements. So the law
   480	  of c mod Q is ≤ (1 + Q/(2H+1))^d ≤ e times uniform.
   481	- **The map mod r.** For r ∈ 𝒫 (r ∤ N since r < B), c ↦ f mod r is an affine bijection from F_r^d onto:
   482	  - if r ∤ g₁: all polynomials of degree ≤ d with f(m_r) = v_r := N g₁^{−d} ≠ 0, where m_r = g₀/g₁;
   483	  - if r | g₁: all polynomials with leading coefficient a_d ≢ 0 and arbitrary lower coefficients. Here
   484	    (g₁x − g₀) ≡ −g₀ is a unit.
   485	- **Counting irreducibles.**
   486	  - Monic irreducible Q of degree d with Q(0) = w ≠ 0 correspond to elements of F_{r^d} of degree d and norm
   487	    (−1)^d w. There are ≥ ((r^d − 1)/(r − 1) − 2r^{d/2})/d ≥ r^{d−1}/(2d) of them for r ≥ 16.
   488	  - Translating x ↦ x + m_r and summing over the r − 1 leading coefficients, the irreducible fraction of the affine
   489	    space is ≥ 1/(4d). In the case r | g₁ it is ≥ 1/(2d).
   490	- **Conclusion.** An irreducible reduction of degree d, with r ∤ f_d, forces f irreducible over Q. By CRT the
   491	  reductions are independent under the uniform law. Finally |𝒫| ≥ log H/(3 log log H). ∎
   492	- **Comparison.**
   493	  - pcest quoted LV's H^{−1/3+o(1)}, via a transfer of LV Lemma 5.1, checked at the level of statements. Codex
   494	    sketches the same order, O(H^{−1/3} log H), by a variance argument.
   495	  - Lemma IR is weaker but fully proved, and it is enough: it is L^{−ω(1)}, while D_k only needs Pr[𝒢ᶜ] ≤ γ/2.
