   522	## 6. The HECM caveat
   523	
   524	### 6.1 What Theorem A needs
   525	- **Smoothness tests occur only inside special-q searches.** These test G(P)/ℓ and F_A(P) for the ≤ C_abs·S²
   526	  points of one lattice box. They also occur in the preprocessing, which is trial division.
   527	- **The shape test** factors u₀, v₀ ≈ √N completely, using LP 1992. LV6 needs no smoothness tests.
   528	- FM counts *every* doubly smooth candidate. A test that recognizes only "most" smooth numbers would require
   529	  redoing FM (and LD) for a restricted set.
   530	
   531	### 6.2 Route 1: batch smoothness (Prop BS) [proved]
   532	
   533	**Lemma BS (Bernstein's smooth parts) [proved; elementary].** Let 𝒫 = ∏_{p≤B} p, let x ≥ 2, and let 2^e ≥ log₂x.
   534	- The B-smooth part of x is gcd(x, (𝒫 mod x)^{2^e} mod x).
   535	- Given x₁, …, x_n, all smooth parts can be computed in O(𝖬(b) log b) bit operations, b = log₂𝒫 + Σ log₂x_i.
   536	- *Proof.*
   537	  - Write x = s·u, with s B-smooth and gcd(u, 𝒫) = 1. Every p^j ∥ s has j ≤ log₂x ≤ 2^e, so s | 𝒫^{2^e}, while
   538	    gcd(u, 𝒫^{2^e}) = 1.
   539	  - Cost: a product tree of the x_i, one reduction of 𝒫 modulo their product, and a remainder tree. Each level
   540	    costs O(𝖬(b)) (standard simultaneous reduction: von zur Gathen–Gerhard, *Modern Computer Algebra*, §10.1–10.3).
   541	    Then e = O(log log x) squarings mod x_i, and a gcd.
   542	  - This is Bernstein, "How to find smooth parts of integers" (unpublished draft, 2004-05-10), Algorithm 2.1 /
   543	    Thms 2.2–2.3. A refereed treatment is Bernstein, "Fast multiplication and its applications" (MSRI Publ. 44,
   544	    CUP 2008), §§20.5–20.7; see also Franke–Kleinjung–Morain–Wirth 2004 §4. We use only the elementary version
   545	    proved here. ∎
   546	- **Attribution.** Bernstein ("How to find small factors of integers", draft 2002, §3) already proposed batch
   547	  smoothness instead of ECM/HECM for rigorous bounds (Dixon, Pomerance 1987, Vallée, LP): "faster, simpler, and
   548	  much easier to prove". We know of no published proof that carries it out. Prop BS applies it per complete
   549	  special-q box, so that FM's count is recognized exactly.
   550	
   551	**Prop BS [proved].** Replace the per-candidate HECM test in the special-q search (pcest §1) as follows:
   552	1. enumerate the whole box (≤ C_abs·S² points, or stop at once if the lattice is irregular, Lemma 1.3);
   553	2. **first** apply the membership, nonzero and size filters of pcest §1 (P ∈ 𝒫_ℓ, G(P) ≠ 0, F_A(P) ≠ 0,
   554	   |G(P)| ≥ MV/32, gcd checks with N). This removes (0, 0) and every zero value. Then batch-test the absolute
   555	   values |G(P)/ℓ| and |F_A(P)| (the value 1 is smooth trivially);
   556	3. output the first candidate, in a fixed order, that passes the filters and is doubly smooth;
   557	4. trial-divide that candidate's two values by the primes ≤ B.
   558	
   559	Properties:
   560	- The search is deterministic given (A, ℓ). It recognizes every counted candidate, so N_ℓ(A) is exactly pcest's
   561	  count, and FM, R, LD and 4.3 apply unchanged.
   562	- Its cost is Õ(B + S²·log N).
   563	- It is a function of (A, ℓ), so (T) is unaffected.
   564	
   565	**Cost in Theorem A.** Per row, the cost changes from (T_shape + T_SQ) to (T_shape + T_SQ + B)·L^{o(1)}. Since
   566	#rows·B = B^{2+o(1)} is the linear-algebra cost already present, **the exponent never changes**. At Theorem A's
   567	optimum, a = θ/√2 < 1/√2 and T_SQ = L^{1/√2+o(1)} ≥ B in any case.
