   590	   parts by AKS. On a composite part n′ that is not a prime power, run stages j = 14, …, ⌈log₂B⌉, with
   591	   v_j = 2^j, w_j = ⌈L_{v_j²}[2/3, c₁]⌉ and
   592	   h_j = ⌈κ·exp((log v_j²)^{1/3}(log log v_j²)^{8/3})·λ(v_j)·λ(X_max)·log(8 log₂X_max)⌉
   593	   curves of Algorithm C with parameters (v_j, w_j).
   594	3. **One adequate stage succeeds.** If n′ is B-smooth, its least prime p ≤ B. Let j* be the first stage with
   595	   v_{j*} ≥ p, and let q be another prime factor of n′.
   596	   - Take x = p² − ½p^{3/2} and y = p^{3/2} ≥ x^{3/5}. Since w_{j*} ≥ L_x[2/3, c₁], LPP I Thm 1.3 gives
   597	     S_w(p) ≥ p^{3/2}exp(−(log p²)^{1/3}(log log p²)^{8/3}) ≥ 2p.
   598	   - Then Prop B gives a per-curve success probability π* ≥ exp(−(log p²)^{1/3}(log log p²)^{8/3})/(C·λ(p)λ(q)).
   599	   - With h_{j*}π* ≥ log(8 log₂X_max), stage j* fails with probability ≤ 1/(8 log₂X_max).
   600	   - Primes p ≤ P₀ were removed in step 1.
   601	4. **All splits.** There are ≤ log₂n ≤ log₂X_max splits. A union bound gives overall failure ≤ 1/8. Finally,
   602	   check that every certified prime factor is ≤ B before declaring n smooth.
   603	5. **Cost.** Stage j costs h_j·w_j·O(log v_j)(log N)² (genus2rh §3.1). The largest stage dominates, giving
   604	   L_{B²}[2/3, c₁]·exp(O((log B)^{1/3+o(1)}))·(log N)^{O(1)} = L_B[2/3, 2^{2/3}c₁ + o(1)](log N)^{O(1)}.
   605	6. **Truncation.** The schedule is finite and deterministic in length (stages up to ⌈log₂B⌉, ≤ log₂X_max splits),
   606	   so the worst-case time is already within the bound and no truncation is needed. (Truncating at 4× the expectation
   607	   would also leave success ≥ 7/8 − 1/4 = 5/8. Our first draft's "2×, ≥ 1/2" was wrong; codex lv6_2.) ∎
   608	
   609	**Status.** This writes out genus2rh §6.3 ("V4, sketch") for the smoothness-test use. It closes the gap that
   610	LPP's unpublished Part III left, for Theorem A's purposes. Its weak point is that genus2rh's Props A/B are internal
   611	and unrefereed. Route 1 does not depend on them, so Theorem A uses Route 1.
