   186	**Counted candidates and the capped search.**
   187	- Put 𝒫_ℓ := {(a, b) : b prime, V/2 < b ≤ V, |a| ≤ V, gcd(a, b) = 1}, and
   188	  Λ_ℓ(g) := {P : ℓ | G(P)}, which has index ℓ when ℓ ∤ g₁.
   189	- The search enumerates Λ_ℓ(g) ∩ [−V, V]² from a Gauss-reduced basis, tests membership in 𝒫_ℓ, and
   190	  tests G(P)/ℓ and F_A(P) for B-smoothness.
   191	  - Primality of b uses a deterministic polynomial-time test (AKS).
   192	  - Smoothness is tested by HECM, as in LV Cor. 3.19.
   193	- N_ℓ(A) := #{P ∈ 𝒫_ℓ ∩ Λ_ℓ(g) : |G(P)| ≥ MV/32, G(P)/ℓ ∈ 𝒮_B, F_A(P) ∈ 𝒮_B}. Each such P is a
   194	  certified descent relation. ℓ² ∤ G(P) holds automatically, because ℓ > B.
   195	  - The **filter |G(P)| ≥ MV/32** was added after codex pcest_2 (§7.2). Without it, candidates with
   196	    |G| as small as ℓ have algebraic values smaller by a polynomial factor. Their smoothness density
   197	    can then exceed ρ_F by L^{Θ(1)}, which breaks Theorem 4.3's comparison. FM only ever uses
   198	    |G| ∈ [MV/32, 2MV], so the filter costs nothing there.
   199	  - A capped randomized smoothness test is repeated O(log N) times, so that a smooth value is
   200	    recognized with probability ≥ 1 − N^{−2}. This costs a polylogarithmic factor.
   201	- **Lemma 1.3 (regularity) [proved].** Suppose λ₁(Λ_ℓ(g)) < √ℓ/(2S), attained at w. Then
   202	  Λ_ℓ(g) ∩ 𝒫_ℓ = ∅. Otherwise #(Λ_ℓ(g) ∩ [−V, V]²) ≤ C_abs·S² for an absolute constant C_abs (e.g. 100).
   203	  *Proof.*
   204	  - w is primitive in Z²: its content e satisfies e ≤ |w| < ℓ, so gcd(e, ℓ) = 1 and w/e ∈ Λ_ℓ(g); by
   205	    minimality e = 1.
   206	  - So w is primitive in Λ_ℓ(g) (index ℓ), and the lattice is the union of the lines jw′ + Zw, j ∈ Z,
   207	    which are parallel to w at mutual distance ℓ/|w| > 2V.
   208	  - Every point of [−V, V]² has norm ≤ √2V < 2V, so it lies on the central line Zw. The only primitive
   209	    points there are ±w, and |w| < V/2 < b for every point of 𝒫_ℓ.
   210	  - In the regular case take a reduced basis (λ₁ ≤ λ₂, λ₁λ₂ ≤ (2/√3)ℓ). The translates of the
   211	    fundamental cell F at the lattice points of K = [−V, V]² are disjoint and lie in K + F. So the count
   212	    is ≤ (area(K) + perimeter(K)·diam F + π diam(F)²)/ℓ ≤ 4S² + O(V/λ₁) + O(λ₂/λ₁) ≤ C_abs S², using
   213	    λ₁ ≥ √ℓ/(2S), V/λ₁ ≤ 2S² and λ₂/λ₁ ≤ (2/√3)ℓ/λ₁² ≤ 8S²/√3. ∎
   214	- So the cap T_SQ := C_abs S²·(log N)^{O(1)} is never binding on a lattice that contains a candidate.
   215	  Irregular lattices need no separate exclusion: they contribute nothing.
   216	
   217	**Sizes [proved arithmetic].** For P ∈ 𝒫_ℓ and g ∈ 𝒢:
   218	- |G(P)| ≤ 2MV, so |G/ℓ| ≤ X_r := 2MV/ℓ = N^{s−1/(4k)}L^{O(1)};
   219	- |F̂_g(P)| = O_d(MV^d);
   220	- |G(P)⟨c, v(P)⟩| ≤ 2dMHV^d, so |F_A(P)| ≤ X_F := C_d MHV^d = N^{s+h+d/(4k)}L^{O(1)}.
   221	
   222	With ρ_r := ϱ(X_r, B) and ρ_F := ϱ(X_F, B), these are exactly hypD's exponents, so
   223	θ = 2s + h + (d−1)/(4k) as in `src/pcest/params.py`.
   224	
   225	## 2. FM in full (regime (B), design (a″_k))
   226	
   227	### 2.1 Cited results, with their exact ranges
   228	
   229	Throughout, x = N^{ξ+o(1)} with ξ = s + h + d/(4k) fixed, y = B = L[1/2, a], and u = log x/log y ≍
   230	(log N/log log N)^{1/2}. So (log x)^K < y < x^{1/log log x} for any fixed K and large N.
   231	
   232	- **(H) Harper, arXiv 1208.5992; LV Facts 8.8–8.12.** The constants are absolute and effective.
   233	  - Fact 8.11 (Harper Prop. 2): for 0 < η < 1/80, y ≤ x^{9/10}, x^η ≤ Q ≤ √x and M₀ = x^η,
   234	      Σ_{M₀≤r≤Q} Σ_{χ*_r} Σ_{t∈[M₀,Q]} φ(t)^{−1} Σ_{χ_t induced by χ*_r} |Ψ(x, y; χ_t)|
   235	        ≪ log^{7/2}x · √Ψ(x, y)·(Q + x^{1/2−η} log²x).
   236	- **(LV8.5) LV Lemma 8.5 and its proof.** Fix ε > 0. For (log x)^K < y < x^{1/log log x},
   237	  x^ε ≤ Q ≤ √Ψ(x, y), ω₀ → ∞ and ω₀ = y^{O(1)}, the majorant
   238	    W(Q) := Σ_{t∈[Q/ω₀, Q], t y-smooth} φ(t)^{−1} Σ_{χ_t ≠ χ₀} |Ψ(x, y; χ_t)|
   239	  (LV eq. (8.2)) satisfies W(Q) ≪ Ψ(x, y)ϱ(Q, y)(H(u)^{−c} + y^{−c}) + Q√Ψ(x, y) log^{7/2}x, where
   240	  H(u) = e^{u/log²(u+1)}.
   241	  - LV's proof bounds W itself, not only the max-residue sum, which W majorizes.
   242	  - The constant is effective; Siegel and Page characters are treated in LV's G₁ part.
   243	- **(T) Tenenbaum (LV Fact 3.16, Cor. 3.17).** If ω(t) ≤ y^c/log(1 + u), then
   244	    Ψ_t(x, y) = (φ(t)/t)Ψ(x, y)(1 + O(log(1+u) log(1+ω(t))/log y))(1 + O(ω(t)/y)).
   245	  In our range the error is o(1) uniformly for t ≤ x, since ω(t) ≤ log x.
   246	- **(Hi) Hildebrand.**
   247	  - LV Fact 3.13: for log y ≥ (log log x)^{5/3+ε} and 1 ≤ z ≤ y^{5/12},
   248	      Ψ(x(1 + 1/z), y) − Ψ(x, y) = (Ψ(x, y)/z)(1 + O(log(u+1)/log y)).
   249	  - Ψ(2x, y) ≤ 2Ψ(x, y) (LV ref. [19, Thm 4]). Hence ϱ(z₂) ≤ 2ϱ(z₁) whenever z₂ ≥ z₁.
   250	- **(C8.16) LV Claim 8.16.** If u → ∞ and c ≥ 0 is fixed, then ϱ(x, y) = ϱ(xy^c, y)(log x)^{O(⌈c⌉)}.
   251	  So ϱ changes by (log N)^{O(1)} = L^{o(1)} when its argument moves by a factor y^{O(1)} = L^{O(1)}.
   252	- **(LV5) LV Lemmas 5.13, 5.18, 5.20.** These concern only the linear map φ_P(c) = ⟨c, v(P)⟩. They do
   253	  not involve m, so they transplant verbatim (§2.3, step 2).
   254	
   255	### 2.2 Lemma 8.5′ (restricted BV for moduli ℓ·(smooth)) [proved; regime (B)]
   256	
   257	**Statement.**
   258	- Let 0 < q < ξ/2 with q > 1/(2k) + η₁, for some fixed η₁ > 0, and Q = N^{q+o(1)}.
   259	- Let Y = 2^{j−1} and 𝒯_Q := {t = ℓs ∈ (Q/2, Q] : ℓ ∈ (Y, 2Y] prime, P⁺(s) ≤ B}.
   260	- Let δ_𝒯 := ϱ(Q/Y, B)/log Y. Then #𝒯_Q ≍ Qδ_𝒯.
   261	- Conclusion: Σ_{t∈𝒯_Q} max_{(r,t)=1} |Ψ(x, B; t, r) − Ψ_t(x, B)/φ(t)| ≪ Ψ(x, B)δ_𝒯·ε_N, where
   262	  ε_N := H(u)^{−c} + B^{−c} + x^{−η₂} for some fixed η₂ > 0.
   263	
   264	*Proof.*
   265	- **Setup.**
   266	  - #𝒯_Q = Σ_ℓ (Ψ(Q/ℓ) − Ψ(Q/2ℓ)).
   267	  - Fact 3.15 gives Ψ(z/2) = 2^{−α}Ψ(z)(1 + o(1)) with α ≥ 1/2, so each difference is ≍ Ψ(Q/ℓ).
   268	  - Summing gives #𝒯_Q ≍ Qδ_𝒯.
   269	  - Since ℓ > B ≥ P⁺(s), ℓ ∤ s and φ(t) = (ℓ − 1)φ(s). Every character mod t is χ_ℓχ_s, and t
   270	    determines (ℓ, s).
   271	  - The left side is at most W′ := Σ_{t∈𝒯_Q} φ(t)^{−1} Σ_{χ≠χ₀ mod t} |Ψ(x, B; χ)|.
   272	- **Part 1: χ_ℓ principal, χ_s ≠ χ₀.**
   273	  - Every B-smooth n is coprime to ℓ, so Ψ(x, B; χ₀^{(ℓ)}χ_s) = Ψ(x, B; χ_s).
   274	  - So this part equals Σ_ℓ (ℓ−1)^{−1} Σ_{s∈(Q/2ℓ, Q/ℓ], smooth} φ(s)^{−1} Σ_{χ_s≠χ₀} |Ψ(x, B; χ_s)|.
   275	  - By positivity this is ≤ Σ_ℓ (ℓ−1)^{−1} W(Q/ℓ), with ω₀ = log x.
   276	  - (LV8.5) applies with Q′ = Q/ℓ = N^{q−1/(2k)+o(1)} ≥ x^ε, where ε := η₁/(2ξ), and Q′ ≤ √Ψ.
   277	  - Hence Part 1 ≪ Ψ(H(u)^{−c} + B^{−c})·Σ_ℓ ϱ(Q/ℓ)/ℓ + Q√Ψ log^{7/2}x·Σ_ℓ ℓ^{−2}.
   278	  - The first sum is ≤ 2ϱ(Q/2Y)Σ_ℓ ℓ^{−1} ≪ δ_𝒯, using (Hi) and Mertens on (Y, 2Y].
   279	  - The second term is ≪ (Q/Y)√Ψ log^{7/2}x ≤ Ψδ_𝒯x^{−η₂}. This holds because Q ≤ x^{1/2−(ξ/2−q)/2ξ}
   280	    and Ψ, δ_𝒯 ≥ x^{−o(1)}.
   281	- **Part 2: χ_ℓ non-principal.**
   282	  - The conductor is ℓ·r′ ≥ Y = N^{1/(2k)+o(1)}.
   283	  - Choose η := min(1/100, 1/(4kξ)). Then Y ≥ x^η = M₀, so all these characters have conductor in
   284	    [M₀, Q], with moduli t ∈ (Q/2, Q] ⊂ [M₀, Q].
   285	  - By positivity, Part 2 is at most the left side of Fact 8.11, which is
   286	      ≪ log^{7/2}x √Ψ (Q + x^{1/2−η} log²x) ≤ Ψδ_𝒯 x^{−η₂},
   287	    using q < ξ/2 and Ψ, δ_𝒯 ≥ x^{−o(1)}.
   288	- The principal character of t contributes exactly Ψ_t(x)/φ(t), which is the subtracted main term. ∎
   289	
   290	*Remarks.*
   229	- **Irreducibility.** The LV Lemma 5.1 transfer reads as in pcest §1, with the affine family
   230	  f̂_{g,w} + (g₁x − g₀)Z[x]_{d−1} and the value condition ĝ(g₀, g₁) ≡ Nw (mod r) for r ∤ g₁Nw.
   231	  - It is still checked only at the level of statements, now for a constant coefficient
   232	    |a₀| ≪_d M + Nw/M^d ≪_d MH·N^{−η₀/2}.
   233	    - This is ≫ H in general. For example, at d = 9, k = 30, μ = 8/75: a₀ ≍ N^{31/200} against H = N^{29/600}
   234	      (codex ldbound_3).
   235	    - The assumption must cover this actual scale.
   236	  - Codex's blind round gives a self-contained finite-field (Turán-type) sieve proof for its version of
   237	    the design: Pr(reducible or degree < d) ≤ H^{−1/4+o(1)} uniformly in the other parameters
   238	    (`notes/ldbound_codex1_report.md` §4). We record it, not as a replacement for the assumption.
   239	  - Reducible f can split as F₁F₂ = Nw without factoring N, so they must be rejected.
   240	- **Relations.** ψ(a − bα) = G(P)/g₁ (mod N), unchanged. There is no w-parity column: w never occurs in a
   241	  relation, and w > B, w ∉ J.
   242	
